# Kart speed in fast turns

The kart's `kart-11` handling lowers its attainable speed during sustained high-speed steering. Full lock near top speed targets 120 km/h, while partial steering has a proportional effect. The effect fades in between 65% and 85% of the normal speed limit. The speed limit can fall by at most 1.7 m/s per second, so steering into a turn does not immediately cut existing momentum. Braking and normal losses may slow the kart faster. Straightening restores the normal limit.

In a full-throttle simulation starting at 140 km/h, the kart travels at 133.88 km/h after one second of full steering and settles at 120.00 km/h; half steering settles at 129.92 km/h. It returns to 140 km/h after straightening. Straight-line acceleration, braking, coasting and reverse match a control with the feature disabled at every tested tick. The default setting is disabled and omitted from existing handling snapshots, preserving other vehicles' identities. The kart's new config hash is `d269fee38dcd927f322731f83f0a4ba069e16fd6741f7d580da4997dbc9294d5`.

The focused regression is `game/tests/test_kart_turn_speed.gd` (18/0); retained kart (42/0), winter-grip (55/0), and selected-handling (3613/0) checks also pass. A separate canonical runner accepted a generated five-lap kart replay of 12,792 ticks in 106,600 ms with the new handling identity; this oval did not reach the limiter. Automated Godot runs used the pinned 4.7.2 executable, isolated local profiles and `scripts/run_no_crash_dialog.py`. The sandbox reported a Windows certificate-store read error; the focused test also reported two leaked instances and a DriveFrame script resource at shutdown. These offline simulations exited successfully. Actual driving feel and physical-device behavior remain unverified. Online races require a coordinated verifier and handling-contract rollout matching the new configuration; no live service was changed.
